          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. What does the diagnosis mean, what does guidance describe as first-line therapy, and which code covers it?",
          "answer": "A diagnosis of type 2 diabetes means that your body's tissues do not respond well to insulin, which is a hormone that helps sugar enter your cells. This is known as insulin resistance (condition-type-2-diabetes). Your pancreas then cannot make enough insulin to overcome this resistance, leading to blood glucose levels that remain above the normal range (condition-type-2-diabetes). This condition develops gradually, often over many years, and many people do not have symptoms when they are diagnosed (condition-type-2-diabetes).\n\nFor first-line treatment, current guidance suggests that the choice depends on whether other health conditions are present (guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy). If there are no other significant health issues, the usual first step is a medication called metformin, along with structured lifestyle changes (guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy). Metformin is often chosen because it has been used for a long time, is inexpensive, does not typically cause weight gain, and carries a low risk of causing low blood sugar (guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy). Your kidney function will be checked before starting and while taking metformin (guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy). Lifestyle changes, especially weight reduction, can improve glucose control and may even lead to remission of type 2 diabetes in some people, particularly if started early. Remission means having normal glucose levels without needing glucose-lowering medication, but it is not a cure (lifestyle-type-2-diabetes-diet).\n\nIn the ICD-10-CM classification system, type 2 diabetes mellitus is coded as E11. This code is part of Chapter 4, which covers endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11). If a record simply states \"diabetes mellitus\" without specifying the type, it defaults to type 2 for coding purposes (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
